    "prose": "Daylight filled the dry upper room. Across it, Ekin saw the sea dragon crouched beside the lighthouse nest, her broad body shining with seawater.\n\nA small shape stirred within the nest. The dragon hatchling was there, no bigger than a cat. Its wings fluttered, but it stayed firmly on its feet, bright-eyed and unhurt.\n\nThe mother dragon shifted sideways. She planted herself across the approach to the nest, blocking Ekin’s path.\n\nThen she lowered her head and placed the brass compass in the nest beside the hatchling.\n\nEkin’s breath caught. She saw the compass clearly, close enough for daylight to flash across its worn brass. She took one quick step forward, but Duru caught her attention with a sharp whisper.\n\n“Wait. Look at the little one.”\n\nDuru had noticed the hatchling. Ekin looked again and saw it watching the compass, then pressing close to its mother’s side.\n\n“That doesn’t make the compass hers,” Ekin said.\n\n“No,” Duru replied. “But the mother is guarding the way.”\n\nEkin searched the room for another approach. Beside the wall, both girls saw a narrow turning stair leading downward toward the lower maintenance room. Far below, visible through the stair opening, daylight and gray sea showed beyond a fixed iron grate.\n\nDuru glanced from the stair back to the dragon. “Don’t rush her. We need to think.”\n\n“My grandfather’s compass is right there.”\n\n“And so is the dragon.”\n\nEkin kept her eyes on the brass circle in the nest. The mother remained across the approach, silent and unmoving. Impatience tightened Ekin’s hands inside her jacket sleeves.\n\n“You can think,” she muttered. “I’m not leaving without it.”",
